-- statement:
--   Working in the Eisenstein integers, the subring ℤ[ω] of ℂ generated by ω=exp(2πi/3), the block defines the norm N(a)=|a|² (with normNat its floor as a natural number), calls an element primary if 3 divides a−1, and calls it a primary prime if it is primary and a prime element. For an element p, the residue classes are the quotient by the ideal generated by p. The cubic residue symbol at p of v is 0 if v is not a unit modulo p; otherwise, if v^((N(p)−1)/3) is congruent modulo p to ω^j for some j∈{0,1,2}, it equals ω^j for the j chosen by the definition, and it is 0 if no such j exists. The additive phase of v at p is exp(2πi(z+z̄)) with z=v/p, that is exp(4πi Re(v/p)). The normalized Gauss sum gaussAtPrime(p) is N(p)^(−1/2) times the sum over residue classes v of the symbol times the phase, each evaluated at a chosen representative of v. With the constant c* = (2π)^(2/3)/(3Γ(2/3)), the prime cutoff sum of a function f at X is the sum of f(p) over primary primes p with N(p)≤X, and the scale is X^(5/6)/log X. FirstMomentStatement is the defined proposition that, as X→∞, the cutoff sum of gaussAtPrime minus (6/5)c* times the scale is o(scale). With θ_ℓ(a)=(a/|a|)^ℓ for an integer ℓ, AngularComparisonStatement says that for every integer ℓ the cutoff sum of θ_ℓ(p)(gaussAtPrime(p)−c*N(p)^(−1/6)) is o(scale). AngularCancellationStatement says that for every nonzero integer ℓ the cutoff sum of θ_ℓ(p)·gaussAtPrime(p) is o(scale). These are defined propositions, not established theorems.
